 "briefing": "# Canva Briefing: Changes from 16 October to 15 November 2026\n\n## Summary\n\nNo verified changes at Canva could be confirmed for the 30-day window from 16 October to 15 November 2026. Every search attempt returned a rate-limit error, and no primary source pages were retrieved. The four categories (news, product launches, hires, and deals) are therefore **unresolved, not confirmed empty**. The absence of results here does not mean Canva had no qualifying announcements in the window.\n\nBefore the 17 November meeting, the newsroom and blog pages listed below should be checked directly for items dated within the window. Any item found there can then be added with its date and URL.\n\n## News\n\nNo dated news items from the window have been verified. Canva's official newsroom has not been reviewed for this period, so no statements on financial results, valuation or share sales, legal or regulatory matters, leadership or policy statements, or market expansion can be reported. The primary page to check is Canva's newsroom [1], with its news listing [2] as the companion page.\n\n## Product Launches\n\nNo product launches, AI features, integrations, or pricing or plan changes from the window have been verified. Canva's homepage and product pages [3] were not accessed. Because Canva publishes product updates frequently, this category is the most likely to contain material items once the primary sources are reviewed. Items should be confirmed as generally available, beta, or limited release before they are cited in the briefing.\n\n## Hires\n\nNo executive appointments, notable leadership hires, board changes, or departures from the window have been verified. Newsroom pages are the most likely primary source for leadership announcements, so they should be checked first. Any hire reported only by secondary press should be treated as unverified until it is matched to a Canva or counterparty announcement.\n\n## Deals\n\nNo acquisitions, partnerships, investments, funding rounds, share sales, or customer or channel agreements from the window have been verified. Counterparty newsrooms should be checked alongside Canva's own pages, since partner announcements often appear there first. No deal sizes, terms, or counterparties can be reported at this stage.\n\n## Recommended Verification Steps\n\n- Review the Canva newsroom and news listing for items dated 16 October to 15 November 2026, recording each headline, date, and URL.\n- Review the Canva homepage, blog, and product pages for launch and pricing announcements in the same window.\n- For any partnership or deal identified, confirm the announcement on the counterparty's newsroom before citing it.\n- Mark any item found only in secondary press as unverified.\n\n## Open Questions for the Meeting\n\n1. Which product launches, AI features, or pricing or plan changes did Canva ship or announce in the past 30 days, and which are generally available versus in beta or limited release?\n2. Which partnerships, channel agreements, or enterprise and education customer deals were signed or announced recently, and how does Canva expect them to affect revenue or distribution over the next two quarters?\n3. What leadership changes or senior hires has Canva made recently, and are there further appointments planned that would affect the teams we work with?\n\n## Sources\n\n[1] Canva Newsroom: https://www.canva.com/newsroom/ (listed as a primary source to check; not accessed for this window)\n[2] Canva Newsroom News: https://www.canva.com/newsroom/news/ (listed as a primary source to check; not accessed for this window)\n[3] Canva Homepage: https://www.canva.com/ (listed as a primary source to check; not accessed for this window)",
